{#
  T-3.02 — the account-area shell.

  Every customer page extends this instead of `layouts.master`, and fills
  `{% block inner_content %}` instead of `{% block content %}`.

  WHAT THE ARTBOARDS SHOW, MEASURED ACROSS FIVE OF THEM. `My Account Page`,
  `Notifications Page`, `Orders In Progress`, `Loyalty Points Page` and
  `Favorites Page` draw the identical shell and agree to the pixel:

    · the header bar, ending at y=186
    · a 32px gap
    · a breadcrumb row `x16..377`, **56 tall**, with a 1px `#646361` rule along
      its bottom edge at y=274 — `M377 274V273H16V274V275H377V274Z`, the same
      path in every file
    · a 32px gap
    · the page's own cards, beginning at y=306 in all five

  WHAT UPSTREAM DREW AND THE DESIGN DOES NOT, both removed here:

    · **the gradient `.profile-header` band.** No artboard has it. Dropping the
      `profile-header gradient-bg` classes is enough — the rules that recolour
      the breadcrumb white live under those selectors in upstream's own
      stylesheets, so they stop applying and nothing had to be edited.
    · **the sticky sidebar carrying `salla-user-menu`.** It is `hidden lg:block`
      — an element absent from the mobile design and appearing only above it,
      which is exactly what B4 forbids at larger breakpoints. **Nothing is lost
      by removing it:** T-3.04's header already carries
      `<salla-user-menu avatar-only show-header show-trigger>`, the same
      component, on *every* page rather than only these. "Consistent navigation
      across all customer pages" is more true after this task than before it.

  THE `<h1>` IS PRESENT AND NOT VISIBLE, AND THAT IS A REAL DECISION. No account
  artboard draws a page heading; the breadcrumb's last item carries the page
  name. A page with no `h1` is poor for both SEO and screen-reader navigation, so
  the heading stays in the document and loses only its pixels — the same trade
  T-7.07 made with `title_hidden` on the story view. A page that *does* draw a
  heading overrides `inner_title`, as `orders/single.twig` already does.

  THE UNAUTHENTICATED BRANCH IS A NET, NOT A REDIRECT. A Twig layout cannot
  redirect; Salla's router owns that, and reimplementing auth is forbidden
  outright. What the theme can guarantee is that account markup is never rendered
  without an account behind it: `user.type` is upstream's own logged-in test —
  `loyalty.twig` and `orders/single.twig` both branch on `== 'user'` — and where
  it does not hold, the page shows the way in rather than an empty shell. It
  opens Salla's own login modal through `login::open`, the event
  `salla-add-product-button` uses, so no auth flow is duplicated here.
#}
{% extends "layouts.master" %}
{% block content %}
    <div class="container">
        <div class="customer-layout">
            <nav class="customer-layout__breadcrumb" aria-label="{{ trans('theme.customer.breadcrumb') }}">
                <salla-breadcrumb></salla-breadcrumb>
            </nav>

            {% block inner_title %}
                <h1 class="sr-only">{{ page.title }}</h1>
            {% endblock %}

            <div class="customer-layout__content">
                {% if user.type == 'user' %}
                    {#
                      All customer pages share this shell; the block below is the
                      only thing that differs between them.
                    #}
                    {% block inner_content %}{% endblock %}
                {% else %}
                    {% include 'components.ui.empty-state' with {
                        icon:         'user',
                        message:      trans('theme.customer.sign_in_required'),
                        action_label: trans('theme.customer.sign_in'),
                        action_attrs: { onclick: "salla.event.dispatch('login::open')" },
                        testid:       'store-customer-guest'
                    } only %}
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
